# What the study of the old body's host-language grammars found (study/r2/gap-host-language-grammars.md).

n: 3.11
name: host-language-grammars
kind: hub
is: The old body's five host-language files (Go, Rust, TypeScript, Prolog, Python: 276 rules, 180 KB) hold flat fixed phrases with no rule references, emit per-language node categories and reverse through hand-written closures to tokens. Their one test shares two integers. A Go, Java and BML one-node test was deleted on 2026-09-29. A 360-line C probe read one rule set both ways: three surfaces gave one node that printed and re-read to itself, at 9 shared rules plus 7 or 8 per language. Type-dependent operators, scope, comments and scale stay open.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-host-language-grammars

n: 3.11.1
name: today
kind: hub
is: What stands in the old body at commit b8ff978c9: five host-language rule files with 276 flat phrase rules, per-language node categories, a Python scanner and layout step that nothing calls, and the body's own audit, which sets full parsing to 0. The cut of 2026-09-29 removed the recursive Go and Java grammars and the Python path that ran text.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-host-language-grammars

