n: 3.11.2
name: rule-forms
kind: hub
is: How the old body holds a grammar rule. The object engine's rule is a token pattern plus two closures, one building the node and one rebuilding tokens: both directions, each written as code. The cursor engine's rule is a pattern and a template as data over text: one direction. The home language's grammar runs on the cursor engine, which carries that language's particulars. The two engines were never joined.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-host-language-grammars

n: 3.11.2.1
name: text-and-object-rule
kind: observation
is: A host rule is written name ::= pattern => action <= reverse; and is split by substring search for those four marks in source-compiler.fk. A rule compiles to a five-element list: name, sequence pattern, action, identity inverse, reverse. Action and reverse are closures over token objects, never text. Rule lines are 12 to 19% of each file's bytes.
status: observed
src: 2.19.8#477-499 2.19.7#2102 2.19.7#2127-2143 2.19.7#5-7 2.19.2#17-21 2.19.1#4
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-host-language-grammars

n: 3.11.2.2
name: cursor-rule
kind: observation
is: The cursor engine in bmf-grammar.fk holds a rule as the list (name pattern template), both parts data. There are 17 pattern forms, among them ref, cap, rep, sep, seq, alt and infix with an operator table, and 10 template forms, among them emit, splice and const. Choice is ordered with no re-entry. The engine has no reverse direction.
status: observed
src: 2.19.12#51 2.19.12#58-59 2.19.12#1109-1137 2.19.12#817-826 2.19.1#3
