at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-host-language-grammars

n: 3.11.4.2
name: lens-definition
kind: finding
is: A host-language lens is a list of rows (rule NAME PATTERN TEMPLATE), pattern and template both data, plus rows (lex NEUTRAL spelling). One interpreter reads by matching the pattern and interning the template, prints by running the template as a destructor and walking the pattern, and translates by reading with one lens and printing with another.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.11.2.2 3.11.2.7 3.11.4.1 3.11.4.4 3.11.4.5
limit: Shown on about 15 constructs in one probe; no full host language was measured. A construct the target lens has no row for is refused; translating it needs a node-to-node recipe, a third artefact the mechanism does not supply.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-host-language-grammars

n: 3.11.4.3
name: inverse-from-content-addressing
kind: finding
is: Nodes are content-addressed, so interning a blueprint with children and reading both back are inverses, and a template built from interning alone needs no second definition to print. A row can print when all variable text is captured, each capture appears exactly once in the template, and parentheses print only when the plain alternatives fail.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 1.3 3.11.4.5 3.11.4.6 3.11.4.8
limit: The study marks the three conditions as inferred and the first as demonstrated. The printing guard, a rule asked to print the node it is already printing answers no, was tried on one grammar.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-host-language-grammars

